    "description": "The WarehousePlatform contains an InventoryRegistry shared by checkout workers, request handlers, and a replenishment service. Checkout calls reserveSku(sku, quantity), while the replenishment service publishes newly received stock. Each reservation must either consume the full requested quantity or change nothing, and the available quantity must never become negative. A checkout request that arrives while the relevant stock record is empty must wait until replenishment changes that state; returning an immediate refusal would lose purchases. The registry may serve many unrelated SKUs, so blocking every caller on one global mutex is unacceptable, and workers must sleep on the affected record rather than poll it. Each record must expose a way for the replenishment service to signal a change, and cancellation or exceptions must not leave waiters stranded. The implementation must keep the invariant and waiting protocol owned by the shared registry rather than scattered across checkout and replenishment code.",
    "decision_statement": "Should each inventory record own the synchronization and waiting mechanism that enforces exact reservations and lets callers wait for replenishment?",
    "notes": "Monitor-object fits the registry owning per-record synchronization and cooperative waiting for replenishment; guarded-suspension and lockable-object are near-misses (predicate waiting, published per-record locks), supported by thread-safe-interface border locking and scoped-locking release safety."
  },
